Naegleria fowleri enters the central nervous system (CNS) through the **olfactory nerve** (cranial nerve I), which connects the nasal cavity to the brain. This is the primary route of infection for this parasite, as it travels from the nasal passages to the olfactory bulb and then to the brain.

The other options (e.g., mastoid sinus, frontal sinus, nasal cavity, etc.) are less likely to be the primary portal of entry for Naegleria fowleri, as the parasite specifically exploits the olfactory pathway.

Thus, the most likely portal of entry is the **olfactory nerve**.

