In a skewed distribution with a tail extended to the left (negatively skewed), the mean is typically pulled in the direction of the tail, making it lower than the median. The median is generally a better measure of central tendency in skewed distributions because it is less affected by extreme values.

The mode, which is the most frequently occurring value, is not necessarily the highest value in this context, but it is often close to the median in skewed distributions.

In this case, the **median** is the most robust measure of central tendency and is less influenced by the skew compared to the mean.

The answer is **(A)**.
